react-native-basics/2/2.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to arrange components across different screen sizes using Flexbox. You will master controlling the flow of items, aligning them along axes, and sharing available screen space dynamically.

## React Native Flexbox basics
React Native uses JavaScript objects to apply styles to components via the `style` prop, with property names written in camelCase (such as `backgroundColor`). Rather than relying on traditional web layout models, React Native uses Flexbox by default for laying out child components.

While Flexbox in React Native functions very similarly to CSS on the web, there are important default differences to remember:
- `flexDirection` defaults to `column` rather than `row`.
- `alignContent` defaults to `flex-start` instead of `stretch`.
- `flexShrink` defaults to `0` instead of `1`.
- The `flex` property only accepts a single number.

## Controlling layout along axes
Flexbox positions elements along two axes: the main axis and the cross axis.

- `flexDirection` sets the main axis. The default is `column` (top to bottom), but you can set it to `row` (left to right), `column-reverse` (bottom to top), or `row-reverse` (right to left).
- `justifyContent` distributes children along the main axis. Options include `flex-start` (the default), `center`, `flex-end`, `space-between`, `space-around`, and `space-evenly`.
- `alignItems` aligns children along the cross axis (the axis perpendicular to the main axis). Choices include `stretch` (the default), `flex-start`, `center`, `flex-end`, and `baseline`. Note that for `stretch` to work, child components must not have a fixed dimension on that cross axis.
- `alignSelf` allows an individual child to override the parent container's `alignItems` rule.

## Distributing space with flex
The `flex` property determines how much available space an item occupies along the main axis relative to its siblings.

When a parent container has a defined size or sets `flex: 1`, its children divide the available space according to their own `flex` values. For example, if three sibling views have `flex: 1`, `flex: 2`, and `flex: 3`, the total sum is 6. The views receive 1/6, 2/6, and 3/6 of the container's space, respectively.

Remember that if a parent view has no fixed `height` or `width`, and no `flex` value, its size will be zero, meaning any flex children inside will not appear.

Here is an example demonstrating these properties in action:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { StyleSheet, View, type ViewStyle } from 'react-native';

type BoxProps = {
  backgroundColor: string;
  flex?: number;
  alignSelf?: ViewStyle['alignSelf'];
};

function Box({ backgroundColor, flex, alignSelf }: BoxProps): React.JSX.Element {
  return (
    <View style={[styles.box, { backgroundColor, flex, alignSelf }]} />
  );
}

export default function LayoutScreen(): React.JSX.Element {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Box backgroundColor="powderblue" flex={1} />
      <Box backgroundColor="skyblue" flex={2} />
      <Box backgroundColor="steelblue" flex={1} alignSelf="center" />
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    flexDirection: 'column',
    padding: 20,
  },
  box: {
    width: 60,
  },
});
```

### Try it
Create a container `View` with `flex: 1` and `flexDirection: 'row'`. Add three child `View` components with distinct background colours and fixed dimensions (such as `width: 50` and `height: 50`). Set `justifyContent: 'space-around'` and `alignItems: 'center'` on the container to see how items align horizontally and vertically.

### Key points
- Flexbox in React Native defaults to `flexDirection: 'column'` rather than `row`.
- `justifyContent` manages positioning along the main axis, while `alignItems` manages alignment along the cross axis.
- An individual child component can override its parent's `alignItems` setting by using `alignSelf`.
- The `flex` property takes a single number that divides available space proportionally among siblings.
- A parent container must have a defined dimension or `flex: 1` for its flex children to be visible.

react-native-basics/2/3.body:
In mobile applications, displaying collections of information is an everyday requirement. You will learn how to render performant scrolling lists using `FlatList` and how to organise grouped items using `SectionList`.

## Why choose FlatList?

When building scrollable content, you might consider using a basic `ScrollView`. However, a `ScrollView` renders all its child components simultaneously, even those far off the screen. For long or changing lists, this quickly consumes memory and harms performance.

React Native provides the `FlatList` component to solve this problem. Instead of rendering everything upfront, `FlatList` only renders the elements currently showing on the screen. It dynamically manages offscreen items to keep memory usage low and scrolling smooth.

## The core props of FlatList

To display a list, `FlatList` requires two essential props:
- `data`: an array containing the information you want to present.
- `renderItem`: a callback function that receives individual items and returns a formatted component.

By default, `FlatList` checks for a `key` or `id` property on each item to track list items and handle re-ordering. If your data uses a different identifier, provide the `keyExtractor` prop to return a unique string key.

```tsx
import { FlatList, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

type User = { id: string; name: string };

const USERS: User[] = [
  { id: 'u1', name: 'Devin' },
  { id: 'u2', name: 'Dominic' },
];

export const UserList = () => (
  <View style={styles.container}>
    <FlatList
      data={USERS}
      renderItem={({ item }: { item: User }) => (
        <Text style={styles.item}>{item.name}</Text>
      )}
      keyExtractor={(item: User) => item.id}
    />
  </View>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, paddingTop: 22 },
  item: { padding: 10, fontSize: 18, height: 44 },
});
```

## Grouping data with SectionList

When your data is broken into logical groups—such as contacts organised alphabetically—use `SectionList`. Instead of a flat array, `SectionList` takes a `sections` prop where each section object contains its own `data` array along with section-level metadata.

You then supply `renderSectionHeader` to display a heading above each subsection alongside the standard `renderItem` prop:

```tsx
import { SectionList, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

type Section = { title: string; data: string[] };

const SECTIONS: Section[] = [
  { title: 'D', data: ['Dan', 'Dominic'] },
  { title: 'J', data: ['James', 'Joel'] },
];

export const GroupedList = () => (
  <View style={styles.container}>
    <SectionList
      sections={SECTIONS}
      renderItem={({ item }: { item: string }) => <Text style={styles.item}>{item}</Text>}
      renderSectionHeader={({ section }: { section: Section }) => (
        <Text style={styles.header}>{section.title}</Text>
      )}
      keyExtractor={(item: string) => item}
    />
  </View>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, paddingTop: 22 },
  header: { fontSize: 14, fontWeight: 'bold', backgroundColor: '#f0f0f0' },
  item: { padding: 10, fontSize: 18 },
});
```

## Helpful list enhancements

`FlatList` provides several props for common interface patterns:
- `ItemSeparatorComponent`: renders a component in between each item, excluding the top and bottom.
- `ListHeaderComponent` and `ListFooterComponent`: attach elements to the top or bottom of all the items.
- `horizontal`: setting this boolean prop to `true` renders items next to each other horizontally instead of vertically.
- `extraData`: tells `FlatList` to re-render when external state changes, as `FlatList` avoids re-rendering when props remain shallow-equal.

### Try it

Create a `FlatList` displaying a list of your favourite books or hobbies. Add an `ItemSeparatorComponent` using a small `<View>` with a border or background colour to place a line between each row.

### Key points

- `FlatList` optimises memory by rendering only the items currently visible in the viewport.
- Every `FlatList` requires a `data` array and a `renderItem` function.
- Use `keyExtractor` to supply a unique string key for each item if your data does not already include `key` or `id`.
- Use `SectionList` when you need to group items under separate section headers.
- Pass `extraData` to `FlatList` when `renderItem` relies on state or variables outside the `data` array.

react-native-basics/2/4.body:
By the end of this lesson, you will be able to capture tap and press interactions in your app using the standard `Button` component, classic `Touchable` wrappers, and the modern `Pressable` component.

## The standard Button component

React Native provides a basic `Button` component that renders nicely across platforms. On iOS, it displays as coloured label text resembling a standard system button. On Android, it renders as a rounded rectangle with light text.

A minimal `Button` example includes `title` for the button label and `onPress` for the function to execute when tapped. You can also specify an optional `color` prop to change the colour of the button.

```tsx
import type { JSX } from 'react';
import { Button, StyleSheet, View } from 'react-native';

export default function App(): JSX.Element {
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Button
        title="Save Changes"
        color="#841584"
        onPress={(): void => console.log('Button pressed')}
      />
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    justifyContent: 'center',
    padding: 20,
  },
});
```

While `Button` is quick to display, it does not provide custom styling options. For bespoke designs, React Native provides dedicated touchable wrappers.

## Classic Touchable components

When a basic button does not look right for your app, you can build your own using touchable components. These components do not provide default styling, but they capture tap gestures and display visual feedback:

- `TouchableOpacity`: Reduces the opacity of the button, allowing the background to show through while pressing down.
- `TouchableHighlight`: Darkens the view's background when pressed down, suitable for use anywhere you would use a button or link on the web.
- `TouchableNativeFeedback`: Displays ink surface reaction ripples on Android in response to touch. This component is specific to Android.
- `TouchableWithoutFeedback`: Handles tap gestures without displaying any visual feedback.

All touchable components accept an `onPress` callback. To detect when someone holds down on an element for a set amount of time, you can pass a function to the `onLongPress` prop.

## Building interactive elements with Pressable

The `Pressable` component is a core wrapper capable of detecting distinct stages of press interactions on any of its children. When a press begins, `onPressIn` is called when activated. Following `onPressIn`, one of two outcomes occurs:

1. The user removes their finger, triggering `onPressOut` followed by `onPress`.
2. The user leaves their finger down longer than 500 milliseconds (customisable using `delayLongPress`), which triggers `onLongPress`. When they eventually remove their finger, `onPressOut` still fires.

A powerful feature of `Pressable` is that both its `style` and `children` props can accept a function receiving an object with a `pressed` boolean. This lets you reactively change appearance without managing manual state.

```tsx
import type { JSX } from 'react';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text } from 'react-native';

export default function ActionCard(): JSX.Element {
  return (
    <Pressable
      onPress={(): void => console.log('Tapped card')}
      style={({ pressed }: { pressed: boolean }) => [
        styles.card,
        { backgroundColor: pressed ? '#d2e6ff' : '#ffffff' },
      ]}
      hitSlop={10}>
      {({ pressed }: { pressed: boolean }): JSX.Element => (
        <Text style={styles.text}>{pressed ? 'Pressed!' : 'Tap me'}</Text>
      )}
    </Pressable>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: {
    padding: 16,
    borderRadius: 8,
    borderWidth: 1,
    borderColor: '#cccccc',
  },
  text: { fontSize: 16 },
});
```

`Pressable` also supports `hitSlop`, which sets additional distance outside of the element where a press can be detected. Note that touch areas never extend past parent view bounds.

### Try it

Create a custom button using `Pressable`. Give it a default background colour, and use the `style` function to change that colour whenever `pressed` is true. Add an `onLongPress` prop that logs a message to the console after holding down on the button.

### Key points

- `Button` provides a basic, platform-adapted component using `title`, `color`, and `onPress`.
- `TouchableOpacity` and `TouchableHighlight` provide ready-made visual feedback effects for custom layouts.
- `Pressable` tracks touch states including `onPressIn`, `onPressOut`, `onPress`, and `onLongPress`.
- `Pressable` accepts a function for `style` and `children` that receives a `pressed` status flag.
- `hitSlop` extends the interactive press detection area beyond the visual bounds of an element.

react-native-basics/3/1.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how Expo Router maps files to application screens, how layout files structure your navigation tree, and how special route conventions organise your project.

## File-based routing in Expo Router

Expo Router uses a file-based routing system. All screens and routes are defined by files and subdirectories located inside the `src/app` directory. Every file placed in this folder has a default export that defines a distinct page in your app, with the exception of special `_layout` files, which export a layout component rather than defining a standalone page.

Every screen also maps directly to a URL path. For example, a file at `src/app/home.tsx` corresponds to the route `/home`. This URL can be accessed directly in the browser address bar on the web, or through deep links on mobile devices.

Components that are not screens—such as reusable buttons, custom hooks, and utility functions—must live outside the `src/app` directory (for instance, in `src/components` or `src/hooks`). If you place non-route components inside `src/app`, Expo Router will attempt to interpret them as distinct routes.

## Initial routes and route groups

Expo Router uses `index.tsx` files to define the default route of any directory:

- `src/app/index.tsx` represents the root route (`/`) and will appear first when you open the app.
- `src/app/profile/index.tsx` matches the route `/profile`.

When you want to organise files into folders without altering the URL path, you can use **route groups**. A route group is created by wrapping a directory name in parentheses:

```tsx
// src/app/(home)/feed.tsx -> matches /feed (not /(home)/feed)
// src/app/(tabs)/index.tsx -> matches /
```

Parentheses tell Expo Router to ignore the directory name in the URL, allowing you to group related screens cleanly or apply shared layout behaviour.

## Layout files and navigators

Directories inside `src/app` can contain a special `_layout.tsx` file. A layout file defines how child screens in that directory relate to each other, rendering a navigator such as `Stack` or `Tabs` before the child routes are loaded.

The root layout at `src/app/_layout.tsx` replaces the traditional `App.tsx` entry point. It is rendered before any screen in the app and handles initialisation logic like loading custom fonts, managing the splash screen, or wrapping screens in context providers:

```tsx
import { Stack } from 'expo-router';

export default function RootLayout() {
  return (
    <Stack>
      <Stack.Screen name="index" options={{ title: 'Home' }} />
      <Stack.Screen name="about" options={{ title: 'About' }} />
    </Stack>
  );
}
```

If you need a layout without a navigator—for instance, to wrap child routes in a shared header and footer without stack history—you can use the `Slot` component from `expo-router`:

```tsx
import { Slot } from 'expo-router';
import { Header } from '@/components/header';
import { Footer } from '@/components/footer';

export default function SimpleLayout() {
  return (
    <>
      <Header />
      <Slot />
      <Footer />
    </>
  );
}
```

### Try it

1. In your project, create a new route group folder named `src/app/(home)`.
2. Inside `src/app/(home)`, create two files: `feed.tsx` and `settings.tsx`, exporting a basic component from each.
3. Verify that the routes correspond to `/feed` and `/settings` rather than `/(home)/feed`.

### Key points

- All screens and routes are defined by files and directories inside `src/app`, while shared components must stay outside this folder.
- The root `src/app/index.tsx` serves as the initial route matching `/`.
- Folder names enclosed in parentheses, such as `(home)`, form route groups that organise files without adding segments to the URL path.
- Special `_layout.tsx` files wrap routes within their directory and configure navigators like `Stack` or `Tabs`, or use `Slot` as a placeholder component for layouts without a navigator.

react-native-basics/3/2.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to pass data between screens in Expo Router and retrieve that information on destination screens using route parameters.

## Sending parameters to a screen

Expo Router uses URL patterns to share data across screens. You can send data by building dynamic route paths, appending URL query parameters, or passing an object into the `Link` component or the `router.navigate` function. Dynamic routes can be linked directly with their full URL string or by supplying a structured navigation object.

When using an object, specify the destination `pathname` alongside a `params` object containing key-value pairs:

```tsx
import { View, Pressable, Text } from 'react-native';
import { Link, useRouter } from 'expo-router';

export default function HomeScreen() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <View>
      <Link href={{ pathname: '/user/[id]', params: { id: 'bacon' } }}>
        View user Bacon
      </Link>
      <Pressable
        onPress={() =>
          router.navigate({ pathname: '/user/[id]', params: { id: 'bacon' } })
        }
      >
        <Text>Go to user imperatively</Text>
      </Pressable>
    </View>
  );
}
```

You can also supply query parameters directly inside a string URL, such as `href="/users?limit=20"`, or as properties inside the `params` object. Any parameter that does not match a dynamic segment variable name is treated as a query parameter.

## Reading parameters with useLocalSearchParams

To access parameters inside the destination screen, use the `useLocalSearchParams` hook. In this course, examples use TypeScript, so you can provide a type definition for the parameters returned by the hook. This ensures that route parameters have predictable types throughout your component code:

```tsx
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';
import { useLocalSearchParams } from 'expo-router';

type UserSearchParams = {
  id: string;
  limit?: string;
};

export default function UserScreen() {
  const { id, limit } = useLocalSearchParams<UserSearchParams>();

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>User ID: {id}</Text>
      <Text>Limit: {limit}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

The hook extracts both dynamic route variables (such as an `id` defined by a `[id].tsx` file) and optional query values (such as `limit`).

## Updating parameters without navigating

Query parameters can be updated without navigating to a new page. Expo Router allows you to update parameters either imperatively or declaratively.

Imperative updates use the `router.setParams` method provided by the `useRouter` hook:

```tsx
import { Pressable, Text } from 'react-native';
import { useRouter } from 'expo-router';

export default function UpdateLimit() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <Pressable onPress={() => router.setParams({ limit: 50 })}>
      <Text>View more users</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
}
```

You can achieve the same behaviour declaratively by rendering a `Link` component that points to the same URL as the current page with updated query parameters:

```tsx
import { Link } from 'expo-router';

export default function UpdateLink() {
  return <Link href="/users?limit=50">View more users</Link>;
}
```

### Try it

Create a component with a `Link` that navigates to a `/users` path with a query parameter of `?limit=10`. Then, set up the receiving screen to read `limit` using `useLocalSearchParams` and display it in a `Text` element.

### Key points

- Parameters can be passed as query strings or within a `params` object using `Link` or `router.navigate`.
- Any key in `params` that does not match a dynamic segment name becomes a query parameter.
- The `useLocalSearchParams` hook reads both dynamic segment variables and query parameters on the target screen.
- Query parameters on the active screen can be updated in place using `router.setParams` or a `Link` with updated parameters.

react-native-basics/3/3.body:
Most mobile applications need to load remote data, whether reading static information from a server or sending updates to an API endpoint. In this lesson, you will learn how to make asynchronous network requests using React Native's built-in `fetch` API, parse JSON data, and manage loading and error states within your components.

## Making requests with fetch

React Native provides the standard `fetch` API out of the box, meaning you do not need to install additional networking libraries. Because network requests take time, `fetch` returns a JavaScript Promise—an object representing an asynchronous operation that will eventually complete or fail.

To make a basic GET request, pass the destination URL to `fetch`:

```ts
fetch('https://reactnative.dev/movies.json');
```

When you need to send data or configure headers, pass an optional options object as the second argument. You can specify the HTTP method, set request headers, and supply a JSON payload using `JSON.stringify`:

```ts
fetch('https://mywebsite.com/endpoint/', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    title: 'New Item',
  }),
});
```

## Handling responses and errors

Networking operations are asynchronous, so modern code uses `async` and `await` with `try...catch` blocks to handle results. The response object returned by `fetch` provides a `.json()` method, which also returns a Promise resolving to the parsed data.

Always catch potential errors during the call. If you omit the `catch` block, any network failure or JSON parsing exception will be dropped silently:

```ts
type Movie = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  releaseYear: string;
};

type MoviesResponse = {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  movies: Movie[];
};

const getMovies = async () => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://reactnative.dev/movies.json');
    const json = (await response.json()) as MoviesResponse;
    return json.movies;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
};
```

## Fetching inside components

In a screen component, you typically coordinate your network call with React state. You track the loaded data alongside a boolean flag to indicate whether the request is still in progress. The network call is commonly triggered inside a `useEffect` hook when the screen mounts.

```tsx
const App = () => {
  const [isLoading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [data, setData] = useState<Movie[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const getMovies = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch('https://reactnative.dev/movies.json');
        const json = (await response.json()) as MoviesResponse;
        setData(json.movies);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };
    getMovies();
  }, []);

  return isLoading ? (
    <ActivityIndicator />
  ) : (
    <FlatList data={data} renderItem={({ item }) => <Text>{item.title}</Text>} />
  );
};
```

## Secure connections by default

Both major mobile platforms enforce secure connections:

- **iOS**: App Transport Security (ATS) requires requests to use HTTPS. Cleartext `http://` URLs fail unless you configure an ATS exception in `Info.plist`.
- **Android**: As of API level 28, cleartext traffic is blocked by default. Insecure connections require modifying the `android:usesCleartextTraffic` attribute in the Android manifest.

React Native also includes built-in support for `XMLHttpRequest` (which underpins libraries like `axios`) and `WebSocket`. Keep in mind that native apps do not share the web browser's concept of CORS restrictions.

### Try it

Create a component that loads and renders movie data:

1. Set up an `isLoading` boolean state initialised to `true` and a `movies` array state initialised to `[]`.
2. In a `useEffect` hook, call `fetch('https://reactnative.dev/movies.json')`.
3. Parse the response with `.json()` and assign `json.movies` to your state.
4. Ensure `setLoading(false)` runs in a `finally` block so the loader disappears even if the request fails.
5. Render an `ActivityIndicator` while loading, or a `FlatList` displaying movie titles once complete.

### Key points

- React Native provides the built-in `fetch` API for making asynchronous network calls without third-party dependencies.
- Always wrap `await fetch(...)` and `await response.json()` in a `try...catch` block to prevent errors from dropping silently.
- Coordinate network requests with `useState` and `useEffect` to manage loading indicators and update the UI when data arrives.
- iOS and Android block cleartext HTTP traffic by default, requiring HTTPS endpoints unless platform configurations explicitly allow cleartext.

ui-design-fundamentals/1/3.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to analyse and apply proven interface patterns from great applications. You will explore how responsive interfaces adapt across screen sizes, how to structure navigation and overflow content, and how to tailor interactions to different input devices.

## Responsive patterns over simple scaling

When studying well-crafted digital products, one clear rule emerges: a small screen should not simply be a shrunk-down version of a desktop interface, and a large display should not merely be a blown-up mobile screen. Great apps adapt their interface elements to make the best use of each form factor.

Designing for smaller screens first ensures that your mobile experience feels natural rather than like an afterthought. When adapting layouts, you might need to apply very different styles to the exact same content to suit different viewports.

## Navigation and progressive disclosure

Examining navigation in successful apps reveals how designers handle limited screen space. Hiding navigation behind a button forces people to take two steps just to browse: opening the menu, then selecting a destination. Until they interact with the control, users are left wondering where they can go.

Great designs use balanced strategies:

- **Visible navigation**: When you have a small number of links, keep them visible even on compact screens.
- **The overflow pattern**: When dealing with many links, keep them on a single line and allow horizontal swiping across the screen edge. Adding a visual cue, such as a subtle gradient or shadow over the truncated edge, indicates that more items exist. Always place the most critical links first.
- **Progressive disclosure**: If space constraints force you to hide links behind a toggle as a last resort, ensure the toggle button has a clear text label. Relying solely on unlabelled icons—such as three horizontal lines or stacked dots—creates "mystery meat" navigation, where users cannot tell what a button does until they tap it.

## Adapting content with carousels and grids

Carousels frequently solve organisational debates rather than user needs, as users often miss content tucked off-screen. However, thoughtful applications use hybrid layouts: displaying items in a swipeable row on compact screens, and expanding them into a multi-column grid on wider screens.

CSS scroll snap properties allow you to create smooth swipeable containers:

```css
@media (max-width: 50em) {
  .cards {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: inline mandatory;
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }
  .cards .card {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    flex-basis: 15em;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
}
```

Crucially, items in an overflow carousel should not fill the entire viewport width. Allowing the next card to peek in from the edge visually signals that there is more content to explore.

## Respecting inputs and interaction

Great interfaces also adapt to how people interact with devices. Equating small screens strictly with touch and large screens with a mouse is inaccurate, as many laptops have touchscreens and tablets can use trackpads. Instead, you can inspect input mechanisms using media queries.

The `pointer: coarse` query identifies less accurate inputs, such as a finger on a touchscreen, allowing you to enlarge interactive elements:

```css
button {
  padding: 0.5em 1em;
}

@media (pointer: coarse) {
  button {
    padding: 1em 2em;
  }
}
```

According to Fitts's Law, larger target areas require less concentration and benefit everyone, so avoid shrinking buttons for fine pointers. In addition, streamline virtual keyboard experiences on touchscreen devices by using descriptive form attributes, such as `type="email"`, `inputmode="numeric"` for whole numbers, and appropriate `autocomplete` values.

### Try it

Open a favourite website on both a phone and a computer. Compare how the primary navigation behaves. Does the mobile view keep links visible, employ an overflow scroller, or tuck items behind a labelled toggle? Notice whether buttons and form inputs adapt to your fingers.

### Key points

- Responsive designs adapt interface elements to each screen size rather than merely shrinking or blowing up layouts.
- Avoid hiding navigation whenever possible; use visible links, horizontal overflow scrolling, or clearly labelled toggles.
- Avoid unlabelled icon buttons to prevent mystery meat navigation.
- Enlarge interactive targets for coarse pointers, remembering that generous tap targets benefit all users.
- Enhance mobile data entry by matching virtual keyboards to inputs with `type`, `inputmode`, and `autocomplete`.

ui-design-fundamentals/2/1.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to select typefaces for your user interfaces, balance visual expression with load performance, and implement fonts cleanly in your stylesheets.

## System fonts and web fonts

A typeface acts as a voice for your written words. In the early days of the web, designers were limited to system fonts already installed on a user's device. Today, web fonts let you select typefaces that match the specific tone and character of your product.

However, some projects have strict rules about font choices. For example, the GOV.UK Design System requires services hosted on the `service.gov.uk` subdomain to use the GDS Transport font, while services hosted outside that domain must use an alternative typeface. When choosing a typeface, you must consider whether you are bound by existing organisation guidelines or free to introduce a new web font.

While web fonts offer creative freedom, system fonts remain extremely fast because they require no network downloads. Using system fonts (such as referencing `system-ui`) provides immediate rendering and can even grant the benefits of modern font features without downloading any extra assets.

## Defining web fonts with `@font-face`

When you decide to use a custom web font, you inform the browser where to find the font files using the `@font-face` CSS rule. For web delivery, modern browsers support the `woff2` format, which provides the best compression and performance gains.

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: Roboto;
  src: url('/fonts/roboto-regular.woff2') format('woff2');
}

body {
  font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;
}
```

Always specify a fallback system font or generic family (such as `sans-serif`) after your custom typeface. If the network is slow or the file fails to arrive, the browser can still display the content clearly using the fallback.

## Balancing loading and performance

Every font file you load adds weight to your page and can increase load times. Because an interface that feels fast is essential to a good user experience, you must manage how font assets load:

- **Preload critical fonts**: You can ask browsers to prioritise font downloads by adding a `<link>` tag in your HTML head with `rel="preload"`, `as="font"`, and `crossorigin`.
- **Control the display swap**: Use the `font-display` descriptor inside your `@font-face` rule to choose how text behaves while downloading. A value of `swap` shows fallback system text first and swaps in the web font once loaded. A value of `fallback` gives the web font a brief time to load before committing to the system font for the remainder of the visit.

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: Roboto;
  src: url('/fonts/roboto-regular.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-display: swap;
}

body {
  font-family: Roboto, sans-serif;
}
```

## Using variable fonts

Traditional typefaces require a separate file for every weight and style you need, such as regular, bold, and extra bold. If you use several variations, those files accumulate and slow down your application.

Variable fonts solve this problem. A single variable font file contains all the design data needed to generate a continuous spectrum of weights and styles. Although one variable font file is slightly larger than a single standard weight file, it is typically much smaller than downloading multiple distinct weight files. Choosing a variable font gives you wide typographic flexibility while keeping file transfers small.

### Try it

Write a CSS snippet that registers a local web font called `OpenSans` using the `woff2` format, configures `font-display: swap` inside the `@font-face` rule, and applies it to your `body` with a `sans-serif` fallback.

### Key points

- Typefaces set the voice for your interface, but you must balance visual character against download speed.
- Use `woff2` as your primary font format because it offers the strongest compression and browser support.
- Manage font rendering using `font-display` within `@font-face` to avoid leaving users with blank screens while fonts download.
- Variable fonts allow multiple weights and styles within a single file, significantly reducing network requests.

ui-design-fundamentals/2/3.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to choose and apply colour intentionally in your interfaces. You will explore how functional colours create predictable interfaces, how to calculate and satisfy accessible contrast requirements, and how to adapt palettes to user preferences.

## Assigning colour by function

When designing user interfaces, colour must not be applied at random. Design systems often divide colours into two categories:

- **Functional colours**: assigned to specific contexts and purposes (such as brand or error) to ensure predictable and consistent interactions.
- **Palette colours**: broad colour groups containing a primary variant alongside lighter tints and darker shades, useful for supporting illustrations or decorative accents.

Rather than hard-coding hexadecimal values everywhere, assign colours based on their role. In CSS, you can organise functional roles using custom properties:

```css
html {
  --page-color: white;
  --ink-color: black;
  --highlight-color: #1d70b8;
}

body {
  background-color: var(--page-color);
  color: var(--ink-color);
}

button {
  background-color: var(--highlight-color);
  color: var(--page-color);
}
```

Using variables tied to purpose ensures that changing your brand or functional palette updates the interface consistently.

## Meeting contrast ratios

Contrast measures the difference in relative luminance between foreground text and its background, expressed on a scale from 1:1 up to 21:1 (pure black on pure white). To satisfy the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) Level AA minimum contrast requirements:

- **Regular-sized text** (under 24px, or under 18.5px bold) must achieve a contrast ratio of at least **4.5:1**.
- **Large-sized text** (at least 24px, or 18.5px when bolded) and essential icons require at least **3:1**.

Logos and purely decorative graphics are exempt from these rules. Rather than calculating relative luminance manually, automated tools such as Chrome's DevTools colour picker, Adobe Color, Color Contrast Analyzer, or Leonardo can calculate contrast ratios and suggest accessible pairings.

## Conveying meaning beyond colour

A common mistake is relying on colour alone to communicate state or action. If an interface directs users to "click the green button to continue" without an icon or descriptive label, people with colour blindness (such as deuteranopia or protanopia) may find it difficult to identify the right action. Similarly, charts and tables must combine colour with text, distinct shapes, or patterns. A quick way to test whether your interface communicates effectively is to review your screen in greyscale.

## Adapting to user preferences

Modern operating systems allow users to express preferences for dark mode or high contrast. You can detect these preferences in CSS using media queries like `prefers-color-scheme` and `prefers-contrast`.

Updating custom properties inside a media query allows you to switch schemes without rewriting your styling rules:

```css
html {
  color-scheme: light;
  --page-color: white;
  --ink-color: black;
  accent-color: red;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  html {
    color-scheme: dark;
    --page-color: black;
    --ink-color: white;
    accent-color: pink;
  }
}
```

You can also tone down photographic brightness in dark mode using CSS filters:

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  img {
    filter: brightness(0.8) contrast(1.2);
  }
}
```

### Try it

1. Pick a primary brand colour and a light background colour.
2. Calculate the contrast ratio between the brand colour and background using a browser colour picker or contrast tool to ensure it meets 4.5:1.
3. Write a CSS rule using `prefers-color-scheme: dark` that inverts the background and text variables, and check that the dark scheme also meets the 4.5:1 threshold.

### Key points

- Assign colours by function and context rather than pasting raw values throughout your stylesheets.
- Regular text requires a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 against its background, while large text and essential icons require 3:1.
- Never rely solely on colour to convey meaning, state, or instructions; always pair colour with text labels, icons, or patterns.
- Use CSS custom properties combined with `prefers-color-scheme` to create adaptable light and dark themes, and `prefers-contrast` to build high contrast themes.

ui-design-fundamentals/3/1.body:
You will be able to plan high-level page layouts, divide space into balanced columns, and build fluid layouts that adapt to any screen size without writing complex rules.

## Start with a single column

When organising the high-level structure of a page—known as a macro layout—always design for smaller screens first. Before adding any multi-column layout styles, ensure that your content flows logically in a default single-column order. 

Starting with a single column guarantees that mobile devices display content cleanly. When screens become wider, placing long blocks of text across the full width makes lines difficult to read. As a rule of thumb, you should aim for no more than 75 characters per line. Setting your main content to take up roughly two-thirds of the container width on desktop helps prevent line lengths from becoming too wide.

## Defining columns with CSS grid

When sufficient screen width is available, you can expand a single-column layout into multiple columns. While you can achieve column layouts using either flexbox or CSS grid, grid is often much simpler for page-level structures.

With flexbox, you must define the sizing rules on both the parent container and each individual child element. With grid, you can encapsulate the entire column structure on the parent container in a single declaration:

```css
@media (min-width: 45em) {
  main {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;
  }
}
```

In this rule, the primary content area automatically receives two-thirds of the available space, while the secondary area receives one-third. 

## Responsive grids without media queries

Media queries are helpful for switching macro layouts at specific breakpoints, such as shifting from one column to two. However, using media queries to arrange collections of components—such as a list of cards—can quickly become tedious to maintain as you add more breakpoints.

Instead of managing multiple breakpoints, you can instruct the browser to calculate the layout automatically using `repeat()`, `auto-fill`, and `minmax()`:

```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15em, 1fr));
  grid-gap: 1em;
}
```

This declaration tells the container to fit as many cards onto a line as will fit, with each card having a minimum width of `15em` and expanding equally with `1fr` to fill remaining space. If you attempt this pattern with flexbox, items on a partially filled final row stretch across the entire width. CSS grid preserves the alignment of both rows and columns.

### Try it

Create a container class named `.card-grid` that displays child cards in a grid. Set `grid-gap` to `1em`, and configure `grid-template-columns` so that cards have a minimum width of `15em` and automatically fill each row while maintaining aligned columns.

### Key points

- Start layouts with a single-column structure for small screens and design for screen sizes rather than specific devices.
- Restrict line lengths for reading comfort, using common layouts like two-thirds width for main content.
- CSS grid encapsulates macro layout rules on the parent container rather than requiring separate rules on every child element.
- Use `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...))` to create fluid, multi-column card layouts without writing multiple media queries.

ui-design-fundamentals/3/2.body:
In UI design, setting margins and padding with random pixel values leads to messy, inconsistent layouts. In this lesson, you will learn how to organise spacing using structured spacing units, distinguish between static and responsive spacing scales, and apply spacing tokens cleanly across your designs.

## Spacing units and scales

Rather than picking arbitrary pixel numbers for every element, design systems define a restricted set of spacing tokens. For example, the U.S. Web Design System (USWDS) builds its unit tokens primarily on multiples of 8px. Smaller units can represent finer increments, such as half-steps or pixel values, while larger spacing scales upward predictably.

Other systems, such as the GOV.UK Design System and the NHS digital service manual, index their spacing scales using numeric units from 0 to 9. Unit 0 removes spacing entirely (0px). In the GOV.UK static scale, units 1 to 6 increase in 5px increments (5px up to 30px), while units 7, 8, and 9 scale in 10px increments (40px, 50px, and 60px). In the NHS system, units use steps such as 4px, 8px, 16px, 24px, and beyond. Restricting your choices to a defined scale ensures visual rhythm across the whole interface and prevents slight visual discrepancies between adjacent components.

## Static and responsive spacing

When designing for different viewports, spacing needs can differ between handheld devices and large monitors:

- **Static spacing**: Keeps the exact same margin or padding across all screen sizes. This is ideal for internal component spacing that should not stretch or shrink, such as the distance between an icon and its label.
- **Responsive spacing**: Adapts automatically based on the device width. For instance, the GOV.UK Design System uses a tablet breakpoint at 640px, while the NHS design system switches to large-screen spacing above 768px. Below the breakpoint, generous margins shrink down so content fits smaller screens without feeling cramped.

Notably, both systems keep the smallest units (units 0 to 3 in GOV.UK, and units 0 to 2 in NHS) identical across all screen sizes. Tiny gaps inside compact components do not need to shrink on mobile devices, whereas large gaps between sections benefit from tightening on narrow viewports.

## Applying spacing in code

Design systems provide consistent ways to apply these spacing tokens in stylesheets and markup, avoiding hard-coded pixel values:

1. **Utility classes**: Class names let you add spacing directly to markup. In the NHS system, classes start with `nhsuk-u-margin-` or `nhsuk-u-padding-`, followed by the unit number (such as `nhsuk-u-margin-9` or `nhsuk-u-padding-right-5`). GOV.UK uses classes starting with `govuk-!-margin-` or `govuk-!-padding-`. USWDS provides utility classes such as `.padding-x-2` or `.margin-x-neg-2`.
2. **Helper functions and mixins**: When writing component stylesheets, design systems supply preprocessor functions and mixins to read token values. For example, GOV.UK provides `govuk-spacing(6)` for static spacing and mixins like `@include govuk-responsive-margin(6, "bottom");` to automate viewport adjustments. In standard CSS without a preprocessor, these correspond to static declarations and media query overrides.
3. **Negative spacing**: When elements need to overlap or pull backwards, spacing systems support negative units. Functions like `govuk-spacing(-3)` or USWDS utility tokens like `neg-2` allow you to apply negative margins systematically.

```css
/* Responsive unit 6 padding and static unit 4 margin in CSS */
.card {
  padding: 20px;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

@media (min-width: 640px) {
  .card {
    padding: 30px;
  }
}

.card__header {
  margin-top: -10px;
}
```

### Try it

Review a layout with a header, a content card, and a button. Decide which spacing tokens should be static (such as a 10px gap between an icon and button text) and which should be responsive (such as a 40px outer page margin that shrinks to 25px on small screens in GOV.UK unit 7). Write down the utility class names or mixin calls you would use to apply them.

### Key points

- Spacing systems replace arbitrary pixel choices with a predictable scale, such as multiples of 8px or indexed units from 0 to 9.
- Static spacing remains unchanged across all screen sizes, while responsive spacing adjusts above breakpoints (such as 640px or 768px).
- Small spacing units remain constant across viewports to preserve internal component alignment.
- Systems provide utility classes, functions, and mixins that support directional overrides and negative values.

ui-design-fundamentals/3/3.body:
When you build a real screen, you bring together macro page structures and micro component layouts. In this lesson, you will learn how to structure a full page from top to bottom and arrange self-contained components within it.

## Structuring the macro page

A full screen needs a clear hierarchy to keep pages consistent and manageable. At the page level, structure is broken down into distinct functional areas:

- **Skip link**: Rendered before the `<header>` element.
- **Header**: Contains branding, an optional service name, and navigation.
- **Width container**: Wraps content to limit the width of the page and holds items like back links or breadcrumbs.
- **Main content**: The primary `<main>` element where your core page content lives.
- **Footer**: Sits at the bottom of the template as a dedicated `<footer>` element.

Wrapping content in a width container limits the width of the page across viewports.

When a layout instead structures page-level columns directly inside the `<body>` element—such as a `<body>` containing only `<main>` and `<aside>` elements—you can use a media query to create a two-column grid when space permits:

```css
@media (min-width: 45em) {
  body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3fr 1fr;
  }
}
```

## Component micro-layouts

Page layouts handle the screen as a whole, but smaller elements inside the page require their own micro-layouts. A media card, a list item, or a section heading should organise its own internal elements without relying on the broader page structure.

Flexbox is ideal for micro-layouts because it allows you to design from the content out. You can define how elements grow or shrink relative to one another while placing constraints on fixed assets:

```css
.media {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1em;
}

.media-illustration {
  flex: 1;
  max-inline-size: 200px;
}

.media-content {
  flex: 3;
}
```

In this component, the illustration takes up one quarter of the available space and the text takes up three quarters, while the image is constrained so that it never exceeds 200 pixels.

## Designing components for any context

A resilient screen design ensures components look balanced whether placed in a wide main area or a narrow sidebar. If a component relies only on viewport width, it cannot know whether it is inside a tight column or a spacious banner.

By using container queries, you declare parent elements as containers to query their inline dimension:

```css
main,
aside {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.media-illustration {
  max-width: 200px;
  margin: auto;
}

@container (min-width: 25em) {
  .media {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1em;
  }

  .media-illustration {
    flex: 1;
  }

  .media-content {
    flex: 3;
  }
}
```

Setting base styles on `.media-illustration` centres the image and restricts its maximum width. When a media object is inside a container narrower than `25em`, the flexbox styles are not applied, so the image and content appear ordered vertically.

When the containing element is wider than `25em`, the container query applies the flexbox layout alongside the flex item sizing (`flex: 1` and `flex: 3`), displaying the image and text side by side. Combining page-level media queries for macro columns with container-aware rules for micro-layouts ensures components adapt cleanly anywhere on the screen.

### Try it

1. Define a page layout that places a `main` column and an `aside` sidebar side by side on wide viewports using CSS grid.
2. Set `container-type: inline-size` on both the `main` and `aside` elements.
3. Add a card component with stacked base styles and a container query at `25em` so it stacks vertically in the narrow sidebar but lays out side by side in the main column.

### Key points

- Page templates divide screens into predictable areas, including a skip link rendered before the header, a header, a width container, main content, and a footer.
- Width containers limit the width of the page and can house items such as back links or breadcrumbs.
- Micro-layouts manage the internal spacing and sizing of individual components using flexbox or CSS grid.
- Combining media queries for macro page columns with container-aware rules for micro-layouts ensures components adapt cleanly anywhere on the screen.

advanced-animations/1/1.body:
Understanding why motion matters is the foundation of high-quality mobile interface engineering. By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain the physical principles that make interface motion believable, describe how React Native declaratively models these transitions, and assess motion choices to avoid triggering adverse vestibular and cognitive reactions.

## Physical believability and interface motion

In the physical world, stationary objects must overcome inertia before they start moving. Similarly, moving objects possess momentum and rarely come to an immediate, abrupt halt. When an interface element jumps instantaneously between states or stops dead with no deceleration, it feels unnatural to the human eye.

Animations allow you to convey physically believable motion within your interface. Easing curves and spring models simulate the natural laws of acceleration and deceleration. React Native provides two complementary animation systems to handle this:

- `Animated` for granular, interactive control of specific values over time or gestures.
- `LayoutAnimation` for animated global layout transactions across render cycles.

`Animated` focuses on declarative relationships between inputs and outputs, rather than manually re-rendering components frame by frame. You define the target value, the easing curve, and the duration, allowing the runtime to drive updates smoothly.

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { Animated, StyleSheet, View } from 'react-native';

export const FadeInCard = (): React.JSX.Element => {
  const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;

  useEffect(() => {
    Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
      toValue: 1,
      duration: 1000,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [fadeAnim]);

  return (
    <Animated.View style={[styles.card, { opacity: fadeAnim }]}>
      <View style={styles.inner} />
    </Animated.View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: { width: 200, height: 100, backgroundColor: 'powderblue' },
  inner: { flex: 1 },
});
```

By default, `Animated.timing()` relies on an `easeInOut` curve that reflects real-world physics: an element gradually accelerates up to full speed and concludes by gradually decelerating to a stop.

## Motion sensitivity and accessibility

While well-crafted motion provides context and polish, excessive or unconstrained movement can severely harm usability. Motion sensitivity is common: over 35% of adults experience vestibular dysfunction by age 40. For these individuals, unwarranted movement across a screen can trigger dizziness, migraine-induced vertigo, or severe nausea.

Motion can also disrupt cognitive processing. People with ADHD and attention deficit disorders can find moving, blinking, or scrolling elements intensely distracting, pulling their attention away from the core task. Furthermore, extreme motion and rapid flickering can trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy. Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) specifically advise against content that flashes more than three times in any one second.

To ensure your animations enhance rather than detract from the user experience, follow these design principles:

1. Question necessity: Determine whether an animation is essential to understanding content or an interaction. If it is purely decorative and non-essential, consider reducing or removing it.
2. Respect duration limits: Any non-essential moving, blinking, or scrolling element that starts automatically must provide mechanisms to pause, stop, or hide the motion if it lasts longer than five seconds.
3. Honour system preferences: Operating systems provide explicit accessibility toggles, such as Reduce Motion on macOS or Remove Animations on Android. Designing your animations to respect these preferences ensures your app remains accessible to everyone.

### Try it

Review an existing transition or entrance animation in your app. Check whether its timing mimics physical momentum with gradual acceleration and deceleration. Then verify how long the animation runs: if it lasts longer than five seconds or loops continuously, plan how you will give users a mechanism to pause or disable it.

### Key points

- Animations convey physically believable motion by accounting for inertia and momentum.
- React Native uses declarative relationships in the `Animated` API to map animated values to styles without regular re-rendering.
- Vestibular disorders affect more than 35% of adults by age 40, meaning excessive movement can cause dizziness or illness.
- Elements must avoid flashing more than three times per second to prevent triggering seizures in photosensitive users.
- Non-essential animations that start automatically and last over five seconds require an option to pause, stop, or hide them.

advanced-animations/1/2.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to choose between spring-driven and timing-driven animations in React Native Reanimated, and how to configure their properties to achieve the exact motion behaviour you need.

## Time-based transitions with withTiming

When you need an animation to complete within an exact period, `withTiming` is the appropriate tool. It creates transitions determined by a specified duration in milliseconds alongside an easing curve.

By default, `withTiming` runs for 300 milliseconds with an easing of `Easing.inOut(Easing.quad)`. Reanimated includes built-in easings such as `linear`, `cubic`, `bounce`, `circle`, `bezier`, and `exp`, as well as modifier helpers like `in`, `out`, and `inOut`.

```tsx
import { useSharedValue, withTiming, Easing } from 'react-native-reanimated';

export function FadeExample() {
  const opacity = useSharedValue<number>(0);

  const fadeIn = () => {
    opacity.value = withTiming(1, {
      duration: 450,
      easing: Easing.out(Easing.cubic),
    });
  };

  return null;
}
```

Timing animations are deterministic: regardless of the distance between the starting value and target value, the transition will always consume the exact duration specified.

## Physics-based motion with withSpring

`withSpring` simulates real-world physical motion rather than sticking to a rigid timeline. Reanimated provides two ways to configure springs: physics-based and duration-based.

### Physics-based springs

A physics-based spring calculates its trajectory using mechanical properties:

- `stiffness`: Defines how bouncy the spring is (defaults to 900).
- `damping`: Determines how quickly the spring slows down and comes to rest (defaults to 120).
- `mass`: The weight of the spring (defaults to 4). Reducing this value makes the animation faster.
- `velocity`: Initial velocity applied to the spring equation (defaults to 0).
- `overshootClamping`: A boolean (defaults to `false`) specifying whether the spring can bounce over the target value.

### Duration-based springs

If you prefer to configure springs using perceptual time while retaining a natural bounce, you can supply `duration` and `dampingRatio` instead. In this mode:

- `duration`: The perceptual duration in milliseconds (defaults to 550). The actual duration is 1.5 times this value.
- `dampingRatio`: How damped the spring is (defaults to 1). A value of 1 represents critical damping (no bounce), values below 1 produce underdamped bouncy movement, and values above 1 yield overdamped motion.

Note that `stiffness` and `damping` cannot be used alongside `duration` and `dampingRatio`. If both are provided, `duration` and `dampingRatio` will override `stiffness` and `damping`.

```tsx
import { useSharedValue, withSpring } from 'react-native-reanimated';

export function SpringExample() {
  const position = useSharedValue<number>(0);

  const animateWithPhysics = () => {
    position.value = withSpring(100, {
      stiffness: 800,
      damping: 90,
      mass: 2,
    });
  };

  const animateWithDuration = () => {
    position.value = withSpring(100, {
      duration: 400,
      dampingRatio: 0.8,
    });
  };

  return null;
}
```

## Callbacks and execution

Both `withSpring` and `withTiming` accept an optional callback as their third argument. This callback receives a `finished` boolean (`false` if cancelled, `true` upon completion) and an optional `current` animatable value. Reanimated automatically workletises this callback so that it runs directly on the UI thread.

### Try it

Create a component with a shared value initialised to 0. Trigger a `withSpring` animation to 200 using a `dampingRatio` of 0.6 and a perceptual duration of 500 ms. Add a completion callback that inspects the `finished` boolean.

### Key points

- `withTiming` moves between values across a predetermined millisecond duration shaped by an easing function.
- `withSpring` models physical movement using either classic physics parameters (`stiffness`, `damping`, `mass`) or perceptual parameters (`duration`, `dampingRatio`).
- Duration-based spring parameters (`duration` and `dampingRatio`) override physics-based parameters (`stiffness` and `damping`) when provided together.
- Both functions support an optional completion callback that is automatically workletised and runs on the UI thread.

advanced-animations/1/3.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how React Native Reanimated uses the UI thread for animations and gestures, how worklets execute code across runtimes, and how data moves between the JavaScript thread and the UI thread.

## The JavaScript thread versus the UI thread

The JavaScript thread (also referred to as the JS thread or RN runtime) is responsible for handling JavaScript code execution in your application. This is the primary environment where your standard React Native application code runs, including state updates and component logic.

The UI thread (also known as the main thread or UI runtime) is responsible for handling user interface updates. Reanimated takes advantage of the UI thread by executing worklets there to calculate view styles and react immediately to events.

## What is a worklet?

A worklet is a short-running JavaScript function that can be copied and executed across different JavaScript runtimes, including the UI runtime. Reanimated uses worklets to calculate view styles and react to events on the UI thread.

You create a worklet by placing the `'worklet'` directive at the top of a function body:

```ts
function logMessage(text: string): void {
  'worklet';
  console.log('UI message:', text);
}
```

The Worklets Babel plugin inspects your code for this directive and workletises the function, converting it into a serialisable object that can be passed to and run on the UI thread. Functions marked with `'worklet'` are not hoisted. Most Reanimated callbacks, such as functions passed to `useAnimatedStyle`, are workletised automatically.

## Scheduling execution across threads

To run a worklet on the UI runtime manually from the JavaScript thread, use `scheduleOnUI`:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { Button, View } from 'react-native';
import { scheduleOnUI } from 'react-native-worklets';

function notifyUI(message: string): void {
  'worklet';
  console.log(`${message} on the UI thread`);
}

export default function App(): React.JSX.Element {
  const handlePress = (): void => {
    scheduleOnUI(notifyUI, 'Hello');
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Button title="Run on UI" onPress={handlePress} />
    </View>
  );
}
```

Conversely, when your code is running inside a worklet on the UI thread and needs to trigger code on the JavaScript thread—such as setting React state or calling a third-party library—you use `scheduleOnRN`. Functions passed to `scheduleOnRN` must be declared in the JavaScript thread scope (in the component body or global scope), not created dynamically inside the worklet.

## Closures and shared values

Worklets are closures that capture variables from their outer scope. However, only variables explicitly referenced in the worklet body are captured. Capturing large objects can lead to performance issues, so extract properties into dedicated variables first:

```ts
type Config = { primaryColour: string; data: number[] };
const config: Config = { primaryColour: '#007aff', data: new Array(1000).fill(0) };

// Bad: capturing the whole object
function badWorklet(): void {
  'worklet';
  console.log(config.primaryColour);
}

// Good: extract the property first
const colour = config.primaryColour;
function goodWorklet(): void {
  'worklet';
  console.log(colour);
}
```

To pass dynamic values between the JavaScript thread and the UI thread, use shared values created with `useSharedValue`. Their data synchronises automatically across both runtimes.

### Try it

Write a custom worklet function that accepts an offset value (`number`) and logs twice that value to the console. Trigger your worklet from a button press handler using `scheduleOnUI`, and verify in your logs that the message runs on the UI thread.

### Key points

- The UI thread handles user interface updates, whilst the JavaScript thread handles general JavaScript execution in the app.
- Worklets are small JavaScript functions marked with `'worklet'` that are converted into serialisable objects by the Babel plugin.
- Use `scheduleOnUI` to invoke worklets on the UI thread, and `scheduleOnRN` from the UI thread to call functions defined in the JavaScript thread scope.
- Worklets capture referenced variables via closures, so extract individual properties from large objects to avoid copying unnecessary data.

advanced-animations/2/1.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to recognise dragging interactions using pan gestures, detect directional swipes with fling gestures, and smoothly animate views using gesture event data alongside Reanimated.

## Tracking movement with pan gestures

A pan gesture recognises continuous dragging across the screen. It activates once a pointer touches the screen and moves beyond an initial threshold. During the gesture, callbacks supply event data such as accumulated displacement (`translationX`, `translationY`), incremental movement since the last frame (`changeX`, `changeY`), and instantaneous velocity (`velocityX`, `velocityY`).

In this course, examples use TypeScript. You attach a pan gesture to an animated element by passing the gesture configuration to a `GestureDetector` component:

```tsx
import Animated, { useSharedValue, useAnimatedStyle, withTiming } from 'react-native-reanimated';
import { GestureDetector, usePanGesture } from 'react-native-gesture-handler';

export function DraggableBox() {
  const translationX = useSharedValue<number>(0);

  const panGesture = usePanGesture({
    onUpdate: (event) => {
      translationX.value = event.translationX;
    },
    onDeactivate: () => {
      translationX.value = withTiming(0, { duration: 150 });
    },
  });

  const animatedStyle = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
    transform: [{ translateX: translationX.value }],
  }));

  return (
    <GestureDetector gesture={panGesture}>
      <Animated.View style={[{ width: 120, height: 120, backgroundColor: '#b58df1' }, animatedStyle]} />
    </GestureDetector>
  );
}
```

Because gesture callbacks are automatically workletised, you can safely mutate shared values directly inside them.

## Handling gesture momentum with withDecay

When releasing a dragged element, you can preserve gesture momentum instead of resetting it abruptly. The `withDecay` animation decelerates a value based on its instantaneous release velocity.

You read `velocityX` or `velocityY` when the gesture completes—such as in `onFinalize`—and pass it directly to `withDecay`. This allows the dragged component to glide naturally to a stop.

## Detecting directional swipes with fling gestures

While a pan tracks continuous movement frame by frame, a fling gesture detects a rapid movement in a specific direction. The fling activates when the gesture is sufficiently long and fast, and it ends when the finger is released. If the touch is lifted before fulfilling the movement requirement, the gesture fails.

You specify acceptable movement paths with the `Directions` object:

```tsx
import Animated, { useSharedValue, useAnimatedStyle, withTiming } from 'react-native-reanimated';
import { GestureDetector, Directions, useFlingGesture } from 'react-native-gesture-handler';

export function FlingCard() {
  const position = useSharedValue<number>(0);

  const flingGesture = useFlingGesture({
    direction: Directions.RIGHT | Directions.LEFT,
    onActivate: () => {
      position.value = withTiming(position.value + 50, { duration: 150 });
    },
  });

  const animatedStyle = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
    transform: [{ translateX: position.value }],
  }));

  return (
    <GestureDetector gesture={flingGesture}>
      <Animated.View style={[{ width: 120, height: 120, backgroundColor: '#b58df1' }, animatedStyle]} />
    </GestureDetector>
  );
}
```

Directions can be combined using the bitwise OR (`|`) operator. You can also configure `numberOfPointers` if a fling should require more than one touch.

## Fine-tuning pan activation

Pan gestures provide extensive configuration options to prevent accidental triggers or resolve conflicts:

- `minDistance`: The travel distance in points required before activation.
- `minVelocity`: The speed threshold in points per second required to activate.
- `activeOffsetX` and `activeOffsetY`: Boundaries along the X or Y axis where fingers can travel without activating the gesture. Moving outside this range triggers activation.
- `failOffsetX` and `failOffsetY`: Boundaries outside of which the gesture fails if it has not yet activated.
- `minPointers` and `maxPointers`: The minimum and maximum number of touches allowed.

For multi-touch interactions on Android, the default behaviour tracks the latest placed finger. You can enable `averageTouches` to average pointer positions into a centre of mass, matching iOS behaviour.

### Try it

Create a draggable box using `usePanGesture`. Set `activeOffsetX` to `[-20, 20]` so horizontal movement must exceed 20 points before triggering, and update `translationX` only while panning.

### Key points

- Pan gestures track continuous dragging, providing `translationX`, `translationY`, and instantaneous velocities.
- Fling gestures detect rapid, directed swipes defined with `Directions` constants such as `Directions.RIGHT` or `Directions.UP`.
- Gesture callbacks are automatically workletised, enabling direct updates to Reanimated shared values without bridging overhead.
- Velocity from pan gestures can feed into `withDecay` to create natural decelerating release animations.
- Threshold properties like `minDistance`, `activeOffsetX`, and `failOffsetY` let you fine-tune exactly when a pan activates or fails.

advanced-animations/2/2.body:
Building an interactive bottom sheet requires tracking user movement, responding to touches, and animating the interface smoothly. By combining `react-native-gesture-handler` and `react-native-reanimated`, you will be able to construct a custom vertical draggable sheet with gesture detection and spring-driven updates.

## Tracking sheet state with shared values

To move a component interactively, you need reactive values that can update without triggering React re-renders. Reanimated provides `useSharedValue` for this purpose.

For a draggable sheet, you track both the translation offset and the active interaction state:

```ts
import { useSharedValue } from 'react-native-reanimated';

type Offset = { x: number; y: number };

const isPressed = useSharedValue<boolean>(false);
const offset = useSharedValue<Offset>({ x: 0, y: 0 });
```

The `offset` value holds the position coordinates, while `isPressed` tracks whether the user is actively dragging the element.

## Handling drag gestures

Gesture Handler provides the `usePanGesture` hook to capture pan interactions. When constructing the gesture, you register lifecycle callbacks:

- `onBegin`: Triggers when initial contact is detected. You can set `isPressed.value = true` here.
- `onUpdate`: Runs repeatedly as the finger moves. It provides an event payload with relative coordinate deltas such as `e.changeY` and `e.changeX`.
- `onFinalize`: Fires when the gesture ends or is cancelled, allowing you to reset `isPressed.value = false`.

```ts
import { usePanGesture } from 'react-native-gesture-handler';

const gesture = usePanGesture({
  onBegin: () => {
    isPressed.value = true;
  },
  onUpdate: (e) => {
    offset.value = {
      x: offset.value.x,
      y: offset.value.y + e.changeY,
    };
  },
  onFinalize: () => {
    isPressed.value = false;
  },
});
```

Adding `e.changeY` to `offset.value.y` ensures that the panel moves vertically in tandem with the touch movement.

## Connecting styles and gesture detectors

To reflect position updates on the screen, connect the shared values to styles using `useAnimatedStyle`. You can also wrap reactive property changes inside `withSpring` for fluid transitions when press states alter.

Once defined, pass the gesture into a `GestureDetector` component wrapping an `Animated.View`, all contained inside `GestureHandlerRootView`:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import { GestureDetector, GestureHandlerRootView } from 'react-native-gesture-handler';
import Animated, { useAnimatedStyle, withSpring } from 'react-native-reanimated';

const animatedStyles = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
  transform: [
    { translateY: offset.value.y },
    { scale: withSpring(isPressed.value ? 1.05 : 1) },
  ],
}));

return (
  <GestureHandlerRootView>
    <GestureDetector gesture={gesture}>
      <Animated.View style={[styles.sheet, animatedStyles]} />
    </GestureDetector>
  </GestureHandlerRootView>
);
```

### Try it

Adjust the `onUpdate` callback in your pan gesture to restrict movement exclusively to the vertical axis by only applying `e.changeY`. Verify that horizontal changes do not alter `offset.value.x` during drag interactions.

### Key points

- Track sheet position and active touch states using `useSharedValue`.
- Use `usePanGesture` lifecycle hooks (`onBegin`, `onUpdate`, and `onFinalize`) to handle drag events.
- Apply incremental displacement from `e.changeY` during `onUpdate` to move the sheet along the Y axis.
- Connect transform properties to shared values with `useAnimatedStyle` and wrap the component with `GestureDetector` inside `GestureHandlerRootView`.

advanced-animations/2/3.body:
You will learn how to configure entering and exiting layout animations in React Native Reanimated to animate elements automatically as they enter or leave the view hierarchy.

## Entering and exiting presets

Reanimated provides predefined animation presets that run when a component mounts or unmounts from the view tree. Rather than driving shared values manually, you attach animation builders directly to an `Animated.View` using the `entering` and `exiting` props.

Preset families include `Fade`, `Bounce`, `Flip`, `Slide`, `Zoom`, `Roll`, `Stretch`, and `LightSpeed`. For example, `FadeInUp` animates opacity and vertical position into view, whilst `SlideOutLeft` translates an element off-screen upon removal.

## Modifying animation behaviour

You can chain modifier methods onto any preset builder to tailor its duration, curve, or physical behaviour:

- **Time-based**: Set length in milliseconds with `.duration(ms)` and shape movement with `.easing(fn)`. Timing defaults to 300 ms.
- **Spring-based**: Calling `.springify()` converts the animation to a physics-based spring. You can further chain `.damping()`, `.mass()`, `.stiffness()`, `.overshootClamping()`, and `.energyThreshold()`. Applying `.springify()` causes time-based modifiers such as `.easing()` to have no effect.
- **Common settings**: Add delays using `.delay(ms)` or `.randomDelay()`, handle accessibility with `.reduceMotion()`, and attach completion handlers with `.withCallback()`.
- **Custom values**: Override default start and end values using `.withInitialValues()` and `.withTargetValues()`. Prefer flat top-level transform properties, such as `{ translateY: 40 }`, over deprecated transform tuple arrays.

Here is an example building customised entering and exiting transitions in TypeScript:

```tsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Button, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import Animated, { FadeInDown, FadeOutDown } from 'react-native-reanimated';

const enteringAnimation = FadeInDown.springify()
  .damping(20)
  .mass(2)
  .withInitialValues({ translateY: 40 });

const exitingAnimation = FadeOutDown.duration(200);

export function Banner(): React.JSX.Element {
  const [visible, setVisible] = useState<boolean>(true);

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Button title="Toggle Banner" onPress={() => setVisible((prev) => !prev)} />
      {visible && (
        <Animated.View
          entering={enteringAnimation}
          exiting={exitingAnimation}
          style={styles.banner}
        />
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, padding: 16 },
  banner: { height: 60, backgroundColor: 'cornflowerblue', borderRadius: 8 },
});
```

## Performance and New Architecture considerations

To ensure optimal rendering performance, define your layout animation builders outside of components or wrap them in `useMemo`. Creating builder instances inline inside render methods forces Reanimated to re-initialise configurations on every render pass.

On the New Architecture, keep two specific behaviours in mind:

1. Reanimated relies on `nativeID` internally to configure entering animations. If a parent component or third-party wrapper overrides `nativeID`, the entering animation will not trigger. Wrapping the animated element in a plain `View` resolves this collision.
2. When removing an un-animated parent container, view flattening can cause the parent to vanish immediately without waiting for its animated children to finish their exiting transitions. You can prevent this by setting `collapsable={false}` on the parent container.

### Try it

Take the banner example and adapt `enteringAnimation` into a `SlideInRight` preset. Chain `.springify()` and `.withInitialValues({ originX: 200 })`, then add an `exiting` transition using `SlideOutLeft.duration(400)`.

### Key points

- Attach predefined builders to the `entering` and `exiting` props of an `Animated.View` to animate mounting and unmounting.
- Chain `.springify()` to enable spring physics; note that time-based modifiers such as `.easing()` have no effect once spring mode is active.
- Define animation builders outside component render cycles or memoise them with `useMemo` for optimal performance.
- Use flat top-level transform properties in `.withInitialValues()` and `.withTargetValues()`, avoiding deprecated tuple arrays.
- Mitigate view flattening on the New Architecture by applying `collapsable={false}` to parents whose children have exiting animations.

advanced-animations/3/1.body:
In this lesson, you will learn how to measure frame drops in React Native, distinguish between JavaScript thread and native UI thread bottlenecks, and inspect system traces to identify what causes animation hitches.

## The 16.67 ms frame budget

To achieve smooth motion, mobile devices render displays at a target of at least 60 frames per second (FPS). This leaves your application at most 16.67 ms to complete all the work needed to produce each frame. If layout, rendering, or script execution takes longer than this window, the device drops a frame, causing visible stutter (often called jank) or unresponsive interactions.

React Native divides this workload across separate threads:

- **JavaScript thread (`mqt_js`)**: Runs your application business logic, processes touch events, executes API callbacks, and triggers state updates. If an expensive computation takes 200 ms, the JavaScript thread locks up and drops around 12 frames, freezing any animation managed by JavaScript.
- **UI thread**: Handles native Android or iOS view measurement, layout, and drawing. Even if the JavaScript thread is locked up, native components like `ScrollView` or native stack transitions can often continue running at 60 FPS on the UI thread.
- **Render thread**: On Android 5.0 and newer, this thread converts view hierarchies into OpenGL commands through operations like `DrawFrame` and queues them for the GPU.

## Observing frame rates with Perf Monitor

A quick way to observe frame performance during development is through the in-app developer menu. Toggling **Show Perf Monitor** displays an overlay showing live figures for both UI FPS and JS FPS.

When testing, keep in mind that running in development mode (`dev=true`) imposes substantial overhead due to runtime warnings and error checks. While the Perf Monitor helps you spot immediate problems during daily development, you must always verify frame rates on release or profileable builds to obtain realistic measurements.

When a touch handler triggers heavy component re-renders, it can starve the JavaScript thread immediately. Deferring non-essential state changes using `requestAnimationFrame` allows touch feedback to complete before running heavy calculations:

```tsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Button, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

type TaskState = { isProcessing: boolean };

export function HeavyActionView(): React.JSX.Element {
  const [status, setStatus] = useState<TaskState>({ isProcessing: false });

  const handlePress = (): void => {
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      setStatus({ isProcessing: true });
    });
  };

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Button title="Execute" onPress={handlePress} />
      <Text>{status.isProcessing ? 'Processing' : 'Idle'}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({ container: { padding: 16 } });
```

## Tracing frame boundaries with Android Studio Profiler

When an animation stutters and you need to find the root cause, recording a system trace gives you detailed thread timings. Connect a physical Android device via USB, open the project in Android Studio, and run the app as profileable.

In the Android Studio Profiler, start the **Capture System Activities** task, perform the animation you want to investigate, and stop the recording. You can inspect the resulting trace directly in Android Studio or export it to tools such as Perfetto.

Inside the trace:

1. Enable the **VSync highlighting** checkbox to display vertical zebra stripes marking each 16 ms frame boundary.
2. Locate your app's process rows to inspect `UI Thread`, `mqt_js`, and `RenderThread`.
3. Identify which thread breaches the boundary. If `mqt_js` is continuously active across the zebra stripes (executing repeated `JSCall` or event emitter events), the bottleneck is in your JavaScript code. If `RenderThread` spends long periods inside `DrawFrame` crossing boundaries, the GPU is overloaded by complex layouts, excessive alpha compositing, or unoptimised view updates.

### Try it

Enable **Show Perf Monitor** from the developer menu in your local build. Trigger a navigation transition or animate a view while dispatching an expensive state update, and check whether the UI frame rate, the JS frame rate, or both drop below 60 FPS.

### Key points

- Maintaining 60 FPS requires all frame work to finish within a 16.67 ms window.
- React Native separates execution into the JavaScript thread, the native UI thread, and the Render thread.
- The Dev Menu's Perf Monitor provides real-time readouts of both UI and JS frame rates.
- System tracing in Android Studio with VSync highlighting reveals whether frame boundary overruns originate in JavaScript or native rendering.
- Accurate performance measurements must be taken on release or profileable builds, as development mode degrades JavaScript performance.

advanced-animations/3/2.body:
In high-performance mobile applications, unnecessary render passes can block the JavaScript thread and trigger dropped frames. You will learn how to identify the causes of redundant renders, memoise components and callbacks, and eliminate cascading updates caused by misplaced state and effects.

## The render and commit cycle

Every screen update in React follows three steps:

1. **Triggering** a render (due to the initial render or a state update in the component or an ancestor).
2. **Rendering** the component (React calls your component functions to determine what needs to be displayed).
3. **Committing** the changes (React applies the minimal necessary updates to the native view hierarchy or DOM).

Rendering is recursive. When an ancestor component's state updates, React by default calls every component nested inside it. If rendering calculations take too long or trigger subsequent state updates, you risk stalling event processing and degrading responsiveness.

## Memoising components and handlers

When a parent component re-renders, you can prevent unchanged children from re-rendering by wrapping them in `memo` (or `React.memo`). A memoised component only re-renders when its props change.

You can also supply a custom comparison function as the second argument to `memo`. If the comparison function returns `true`, the component will skip re-rendering.

```tsx
import React, { memo, useCallback } from 'react';
import { FlatList, Text, View } from 'react-native';

type Item = { id: string; title: string };

const ListItem = memo(
  ({ title }: { title: string }) => (
    <View>
      <Text>{title}</Text>
    </View>
  ),
  (prevProps, nextProps) => prevProps.title === nextProps.title
);

export function ItemList({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
  const renderItem = useCallback(
    ({ item }: { item: Item }) => <ListItem title={item.title} />,
    []
  );

  return <FlatList data={items} renderItem={renderItem} />;
}
```

Notice the use of `useCallback` for `renderItem`. If you pass an anonymous inline function directly to `renderItem`, a new function instance is created on every render of the parent list, invalidating prop equality and triggering avoidable re-renders.

## Eliminating cascading updates

A frequent source of redundant renders is updating state inside a `useEffect` hook to transform data. When state updates inside an effect:

1. React calls your components to calculate the initial UI.
2. React commits those changes to the screen.
3. React runs your effect, which immediately updates state with `setState`.
4. This state update restarts the entire render and commit process from scratch.

If a value can be computed from existing props or state, do not store it in state or update it inside an effect. Calculate it directly during rendering:

```tsx
import React, { useMemo, useState } from 'react';

type Todo = { id: number; text: string; completed: boolean };

export function TodoList({ todos, filter }: { todos: Todo[]; filter: string }) {
  const [newTodo, setNewTodo] = useState<string>('');

  // Calculated during rendering; memoised if computation is expensive
  const visibleTodos = useMemo(() => {
    return todos.filter((todo) => todo.text.includes(filter));
  }, [todos, filter]);

  return null;
}
```

Wrapping the calculation in `useMemo` caches the result and skips recalculating it during subsequent renders unless `todos` or `filter` change. If the calculation is not expensive, running it directly without `useMemo` is sufficient.

Similarly, avoid chaining effects where one effect updates state that triggers another effect. Update all related states in the initiating event handler so that React can batch the updates into a single render pass.

### Try it

Review an existing list component in your application. Wrap the list item component in `memo` and extract its `renderItem` callback into a `useCallback` hook outside of the JSX. Check whether any values passed to the list item can be calculated during the render pass rather than stored in a separate `useState` variable.

### Key points

- React re-renders components recursively whenever their state or an ancestor's state updates.
- Wrap child components in `memo` to skip rendering when their props remain unchanged.
- Use `useCallback` to avoid passing freshly instantiated anonymous functions as props on each render.
- Calculate derived values during rendering rather than updating secondary state inside `useEffect`.
